    "weight_traffic_definition": "Shader-requested Q4 weight bytes: ceil(m/32) times packed n*k/2 plus f16 scale and bias n*(k/64)*4. This counts every 32-row workgroup load, not unique DRAM bytes after cache.",
    "device_bandwidth_reference_GB_s": 57.5,
    "device_bandwidth_reference": "Same-host 76 MB CastF32F16 p50 from receipts/2026-09-09-q4-gemv-bandwidth/verdict.json.",
    "dense_reference": "Diagnostics-wheel MatmulF32Coopmat, identical (m,k,n), 8 warmups + 16 measured dispatches; GPU slot is next dispatch t0 minus target t0.",
    "conclusion": "Large QMM shapes request only 6.57-9.38 GB/s (11-16% of the 57.5 GB/s practical device ceiling), while the candidate reaches 2.04-2.80x the same-shape dense-f32 TFLOP/s. Dense f32 is itself weight-traffic limited and is not a pure matrix-unit roof. QMM is therefore not DRAM-bandwidth-bound; the response to halving barrier frequency and splitting work over two subgroups identifies shared staging/barrier/issue throughput as the active bound. The n=128 shape is dispatch-latency/synchronization dominated."
  },
